#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153452 :tutorial/content "In a previous lesson, we saw that congruences obey another scalar multiplication property:\n\n$$\na\\equiv b \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nThe (second) **division property** of modular arithmetic tells us that\n\n$$\nka\\equiv kb\\quad(\\textrm{mod} \\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\, n)\n$$\n\n*provided that $k$ and $n$ are coprime*. In other words, we require\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(k,n)=1\n$$\n\n**Caution:** The condition that $k$ and $n$ are coprime is *very* important! The division property does *not* work when $k$ and $n$ are not coprime!\n\nThis might seem a little puzzling, so let's look at some concrete examples:\n\n- Consider the following true statement:\n$12 \\equiv 2 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:5)$\nNotice that the two numbers in our congruence have a common factor of ${\\color{blue}2}{:}$\n${\\color{blue}2} \\cdot 6 \\equiv {\\color{blue}2} \\cdot 1 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:5)$\nSince $\\textrm{gcd}({\\color{blue}2},5)=1$, we can cancel out ${\\color{blue}2}$ from both sides, keeping the modulus unchanged.\n$6 \\equiv 1 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:5)$\n- Next, consider the following true statement:\n$12 \\equiv 6 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:6)$\nNotice that the two numbers in our congruence, *and the modulus*, have a common factor of ${\\color{red}3}{:}$\n${\\color{red}3} \\cdot 4 \\equiv {\\color{red}3} \\cdot 2 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:6)$\nBecause $\\textrm{gcd}({\\color{red}3},6) = 3 \\neq 1$, we *cannot* cancel out ${\\color{red}3}$ from both sides. If we do, we get the following *false* statement!\n$4\\equiv 2 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:6)$.\nIn such cases, we should use the first division property to simplify the congruence." :tutorial/id #uuid "b53a59cc-bae8-5427-90c1-8c6ec21119a6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9018 :tutorial/title "The Second Division Property"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153453 :tutorial/content "In a previous lesson, we saw that congruences obey the following scalar multiplication property:\n\n$$\na\\equiv b \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,kn)\n$$\n\nIt turns out that for\n\n$$\nk\\neq 0\n$$\n\nwe can perform this operation in reverse. That is,\n\n$$\nka \\equiv kb \\quad(\\text{mod}\\,kn) \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nTherefore, for\n\n$$\nk\\neq 0\n$$\n\nwe have the following **division property,** which we can write as an equivalence:\n\n$$\nka \\equiv kb \\quad(\\text{mod}\\,kn) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the following true statement:\n\n$$\n12 \\equiv 6 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:6)\n$$\n\nNote that all three numbers in our congruence have a common factor of ${\\color{blue}3}$.\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3} \\cdot 4 \\equiv {\\color{blue}3} \\cdot 2 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:{\\color{blue}3} \\cdot 2)\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the division property, we can cancel a factor of ${\\color{blue}3}$ from both sides and the modulus.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\cdot 4≡3 \\cdot 2 & (mod3 \\cdot 2) \\\\ 4≡2 & (mod2)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nIt's important to note that the division property does not hold if $k=0$. For example, consider the following counterexample.\n\n$$\n0\\cdot 2 \\equiv 0\\cdot 1 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad 2\\equiv 1 \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,2)\n$$\n\nThe antecedent is true, the consequent is false, and hence the implication is *false*." :tutorial/id #uuid "80aec645-9a36-5a86-a866-dedff95e8091" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9019 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153454 :tutorial/content "Let's see why the division properties work.\n\n- The proof of the first property is straightforward:\n$ka≡kb(modkn) \\mid ⟺kn\\begin{vmatrix}(ka - kb) \\\\ ⟺kn\\end{vmatrix}k(a - b); ⟺kn\\begin{vmatrix}k(a - b) \\\\ ⟺n\\end{vmatrix}(a - b); ⟺a≡b(modn)$\n\n- To prove the second division property, suppose that $\\textrm{gcd}(k,n)=1$ and ${\\color{blue}k}a\\equiv {\\color{blue}k}b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\,n)$. Then, according to the definition of congruent integers, we obtain\n$ka≡kb(modn) \\mid ⟺n\\begin{vmatrix}(ka - kb) \\\\ ⟺n\\end{vmatrix}k(a - b)$.\nSince $k$ and $n$ are coprime and $k(a - b)$ is divisible by $n$, we have that $(a - b)$ must be divisible by $n$. Now, using the definition again, we get\n$n \\mid (a - b) \\mid ⟺a≡b(modn)$." :tutorial/id #uuid "fe50ad20-f759-5095-acdc-f9e5e8d0926d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9021 :tutorial/title "Proving the Division Properties"}]}
